 than other implants so  there were forty eight thousand women in  this country alone  that were victims of this scandal they  waited five years for any kind of  financial compensation they got three  thousand pounds which would not even  cover the cost of the original the  original operation and Jane Smith as  just one example of these women has had  to have five further operations to try  and correct some of the the impact of  having this toxic substance leaked into  her body so we can raise the question  we've got all these sort of ideological  words like authentic or confidence or  you know boosting self-esteem we go I  would like to raise the question is Jane  Smith a more authentic person now with  this toxic material in her body that can  never be fully got rid of that as that  has affected her ability to work her  ability to raise children her ability to  function in the world not to mention all  the psychological impacts as well as the  physical impacts and I think we really  have to look at get away from some of  the euphemistic language that tends to  be used in these discussions and start  to think about whose interest is this  serving who's making huge profits out of  this what are the implications in terms  of sexual politics because we know that  it is overwhelmingly men who are  carrying out these procedures and women  90 in this country 90% of all cosmetic  surgery is carried out on women's bodies  so those are the kinds of questions I  think we should be looking at and then  really questioning what do we mean by  this notion of authenticity for more  debates talks and interviews subscribe  today to the Institute of Arts in ideas  her III TV  you

